From f1702dd10f2458526b8ee5ebde58d45d4af5eebd Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Nipuna H Herath <nipunaherath@hotmail.com>
Date: Mon, 23 Nov 2026 11:11:30 +0800
Subject: [PATCH 04/10] CUDA/HIP: Inkling 2 loop controllers as one batch,
 without the concatenation

Each loop controller of a generated token ran three kernels: CONCAT of the
normalized input and message (4,608 values), the 64-row down product, and
the gated mix (ctrl_mix). The down product launched 64 blocks on a 64-CU
card and ran at ~100 GB/s. A looped layer pass has four controllers, the
attention Q, K and V ones next to each other in the graph.

Up to four consecutive controllers now run as two launches: all their down
products (one block per row and controller, reading the two halves from
where they are, so the concatenation is never made), then all their mixes.
The down product repeats mul_mat_vec_f's arithmetic for the same block size
(the HIP BF16 path, its reduction order), and the mix ctrl_mix's, so the
output is bit-identical. A controller may not read what another in the batch
writes, and every output is checked against every input and other output.

R9700 Inkling-2-Mini UD-Q8_K_XL: 1,732 -> 1,500 kernels per generated token;
tg128 57.3 -> 59.0 tok/s (+3.0%). KLD against the previous commit,
wiki.test.raw -c 512, -ub 512 and -ub 1: 0. New test-backend-ops case
CTRL_BATCH (1, 3 and 4 controllers, shared and separate inputs).
